Planex undo_via_graph — Relation necessity proof (ADR-0002)
================================================================
Validates: undo-via-graph uses Relation to scope snapshots.

Setup:
  count = 0, unrelated = 999
  inc TRIGGERS count (bound)
  dec TRIGGERS count (bound)
  unrelated: NO Closure triggers it
  undo enabled: true

[test 1] trigger inc 3 times — count goes 0->3, unrelated stays 999 ... PASS
        count=3, unrelated=999, undo_count=3
[test 2] px_undo() — count goes 3->2 ... PASS
        count=2, restored=1, undo_count=2
[test 3] undo twice more — count 2->1->0 ... PASS
        count=0, undo_count=0
[test 4] undo with empty stack returns 0 ... PASS (returned 0)
[test 5] trigger dec (count 0->-1), undo restores to 0 ... PASS
[test 6] disable undo, trigger doesn't snapshot ... PASS (count=2, undo_count=0)
[test 7] re-enable, inc then dec, undo reverses dec only ... PASS

[final] unrelated Estimate value across all operations: 999
       (expected 999.0 — never touched by undo) ✓

=== undo_via_graph proof complete ===

What this proves (ADR-0002):
  1. px_undo_record uses Relation graph to find affected Estimates
  2. Only Estimates reachable via TRIGGERS are snapshotted
  3. Unrelated Estimates are NEVER touched by undo
  4. Undo stack works (push, pop, count, clear)
  5. Undo can be enabled/disabled globally

Why Solid.js cannot do this:
  Solid tracks dependencies PER-EFFECT. Each effect knows its
  sources, but there is no global query 'which effects depend
  on this signal?'. To do undo-via-graph in Solid, you must:
    (a) Snapshot everything (Redux-style, expensive), OR
    (b) Maintain a separate dependency index (= rebuilding Relation)

Planex's Relation is a globally queryable graph. Undo-via-graph
is a natural consequence. Relation is NECESSARY, not just convenient.

This closes ADR-0002: Relation's necessity is PROVEN.
